# Google Health

Nocturne can import steps, heart rate, weight and sleep from Google Health. Each Nocturne
server connects through one Google OAuth client that its operator sets up once; the people
using the server only click **Connect**.

## Connecting your Google Health data

1. In the Google Health app on your phone, make sure the data you want is there and linked to
   your Google account.
2. In Nocturne, open **Settings > Connectors & Apps** and choose **Google Health**.
3. Click **Connect Google Health**, pick your Google account, and allow the data Nocturne asks
   to read.
4. Back in Nocturne, click **Refresh inventory** to see what Google has, choose the data types to
   import, and click **Save selection and import**.

Nocturne then syncs about every 15 minutes. It imports the last seven days first; set
**Import data from** to an earlier date to bring in older history, which arrives a month at a
time in the background.

Access is read-only: Nocturne never writes to Google Health.

<Callout type="info" title="Google Health is not enabled on this server">

If Nocturne says your operator hasn't enabled Google Health, its server-level Google client
configuration is missing or invalid. This can also happen after an update to a previously
working server. Whoever runs your Nocturne server needs to check the setup and recovery steps
below before you can connect again.

</Callout>

### Disconnecting and deleting

**Disconnect** stops syncing and revokes Nocturne's access in Google. If the same Google account
is still connected in another tenant on this server, Nocturne keeps that access so the other
tenant keeps syncing; it is revoked when the last tenant disconnects. Data already imported stays
in Nocturne. Once disconnected, **Delete imported Google Health data** removes everything that
came from Google Health; your data in Google is not touched.

## Setting up Google Health on your server

This part is for the person who runs the Nocturne server. If you self-host Nocturne, that is you.

### 1. Create the Google OAuth client

1. In [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), create a project (or reuse one)
   and enable the **Google Health API**.
2. Under **Google Auth Platform**, configure the consent screen: app name, support email, and
   audience **External**.
3. Under **Clients**, create an OAuth client of type **Web application**.
4. Add this **Authorized redirect URI**, with your `BASE_DOMAIN` in place of the example:

```
https://nocturne.example.com/api/v4/google-health/oauth/callback
```

Every tenant uses that same address; Nocturne sends each person back to their own subdomain
afterwards.

5. Copy the **Client ID** and **Client secret**.

### 2. Give Nocturne the client

Set these in your API server's environment. With Docker Compose, set them in your `.env` and
ensure your Compose file passes them to the `nocturne-api` service:

| Variable                      | Value                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
| ----------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_ID`     | The client ID, ending in `.apps.googleusercontent.com`.                                                                                                                                                                      |
| `GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_SECRET` | The client secret.                                                                                                                                                                                                           |
| `GOOGLE_HEALTH_REDIRECT_URI`  | Optional. Only needed when Nocturne cannot work the address out from `BASE_DOMAIN`, for example when `BASE_DOMAIN` is unset. It must end in `/api/v4/google-health/oauth/callback` and match the URI you registered exactly. |

Use the public HTTPS hostname and port. For example, a server exposed on port `8450` uses
`https://nocturne.example.com:8450/api/v4/google-health/oauth/callback`. Set
`GOOGLE_HEALTH_REDIRECT_URI` explicitly if the derived address would omit your public port.
Register this exact address under **Authorized redirect URIs** in Google Cloud; **Authorized
JavaScript origins** do not replace the redirect list.

After changing the environment, recreate the API container:

```sh
docker compose up -d --force-recreate nocturne-api
```

Restarting the existing container alone does not load changed environment variables. These
credentials are server settings; the tenant's Google Health page has no client-secret form.

The **Connect Google Health** button appears for everyone on the server once the client ID and
secret are set and Nocturne has a redirect URI, from `BASE_DOMAIN` or the variable.

### Recovery: Reconnect is requested but the button is missing

The page can show **Google access expired or was revoked. Reconnect.** with technical code
`reconnect_required`, while also showing **Unavailable** and saying the operator has not enabled
Google Health. The reconnect message describes a stored connection error. **Unavailable** means
the API cannot currently use its server-level Google client configuration, so the Connect button
is hidden until that configuration is repaired.

1. Check `GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_HEALTH_CLIENT_SECRET`, and
   `GOOGLE_HEALTH_REDIRECT_URI` in the running API's environment. The client ID must end in
   `.apps.googleusercontent.com`, the secret must be present, and the redirect must be a valid
   public HTTPS URL with the path `/api/v4/google-health/oauth/callback`.
2. Older setups may still use `/settings/connectors/google-health/callback`. That path is not
   accepted by the current server configuration. Update the server setting and add the new API
   callback URL to **Authorized redirect URIs** in the same Google Cloud OAuth client. Match the
   scheme, hostname, port, and path exactly.
3. Recreate the API container with the corrected environment as shown above, then reload the
   Google Health page. If **Unavailable** remains, check that the variables reached the API and
   inspect the server logs without sharing client secrets or tokens.
4. When **Connect Google Health** appears, connect again, refresh the inventory, review the data
   types, and save the import selection. If Google reports `redirect_uri_mismatch`, compare the
   configured redirect and Google's registered URI again.

Your imported history is kept. An import-cursor reset re-reads historical data; it does not repair
OAuth credentials or an invalid callback URL, so it is not the remedy for this connection block.

### 3. Who can connect

Google limits what an unverified app can do, and these limits apply to your client:

- While the consent screen's publishing status is **Testing**, only the Google accounts you add
  as test users can connect, and Google expires their access after **7 days**. Each person then
  has to connect again.
- An app that is in production but not verified can be used by at most **100 users**.
- Serving more than 100 users requires Google's verification, which for health data includes a
  **third-party security assessment**.

For a server shared by family or a few friends, publishing to production and staying under 100
users is usually enough.

<Callout type="warning" title="Changing the client">

A Google connection belongs to the client it was made with. If you replace the client ID,
everyone who had connected sees **Reconnect** and has to click **Connect Google Health** again.
Their imported data is kept.

</Callout>
